// queries.memql
//
// Deployment-namespace READS -- the deploy record queries the DevOps deployment
// bundle owns (epic #2212, I11 #2225). Relocated from dsl/cluster/queries.memql
// into the cohesive `deployment` pack alongside the concepts, shapes, and
// mutations of the deploy data model. The concept ids are held stable
// (v1:cluster:deployment / v1:cluster:deploymentNodeSpec -- see concepts.memql),
// so only the import path moved (`use deployment.concepts/shapes.{ ... }`); the
// query NAMES are unchanged, so component/deploycontrol (deploymentById,
// deploymentsForCluster), component/node/reconciler.go (supersededDeployments),
// the cockpit Deployments view, and the SDK keep resolving them by name.
//
// SCOPE NOTE. The `query node` topology lookups that filter on a node's
// payload.deploymentId (nodesForDeployment / nodesNotInDeployment) stay in
// dsl/cluster/queries.memql -- they read the v1:cluster:node concept (cluster
// topology), not the deployment record, so they belong with the cluster
// concept they project. Only the deployment-RECORD reads move here.

// ---- deploymentsForCluster ----

// Deployment history for a cluster (#1872). Returns ONE row per
// deployment -- the LATEST payload version per deploymentId via
// `asOf latest`, so each entry reflects the deployment's CURRENT
// status (pending/in_progress/succeeded/failed/superseded/rolled_back).
// The deployment store is append-only (one row appended per status
// transition under the same concept id = deploymentId), so a plain scan
// would return every transition; asOf latest collapses to the current
// state. Ordering newest-first by createdAt is applied by the consumer
// (cockpit Deployments view), mirroring clusterNodes' "CLI dedupes
// + sorts" note -- the MemQL query planner has no order/limit clause.

/// Deployment history for a cluster: latest-per-deploymentId rows (asOf latest -> current status
/// per deployment) filtered to a clusterId. Backs the cockpit Deployments history list (consumer
/// sorts newest-first by createdAt). #1872.
@unbounded("deployments for a cluster -- a bounded topology set consumed whole")
query deployment deploymentsForCluster {
  args {
    clusterId  string!
  }
  filter  clusterId == args.clusterId
  shape   deploymentFull
  asOf    latest
}

// ---- deploymentById ----

// Current state of a single deployment (#1872).
// Filters on the row id: createDeployment stamps `id: args.deploymentId`,
// so the row intrinsic IS the deploymentId and it is the leading column of
// the primary key -- PRIMARY KEY (id, "createdAt") -- rather than a JSON
// payload probe. The caller still passes the bare deploymentId --
// resolveFullId (component/memql/executor_filter.go) expands a colon-free
// id against the query's bound concept, which ensureBoundConceptFilter
// folds into the filter of every signature-bound struct-form query.
//
// This previously filtered payload.deploymentId, justified by a comment
// claiming the row id "would need partition+concept prefixing". That was
// stale on both counts: the {partition}: prefix was retired in #56, and
// the concept prefix is composed by the engine, not the caller (#2784).
//
// The payload field STAYS, on two verified readers: node/reconciler.go
// reads deploymentId off the payload, and deploymentFull projects it. So
// deleting it is a consumer-visible change this fix has no business
// forcing. (An earlier draft of this comment also claimed
// deploymentNodeSpec keys on it as an FK and that previousDeploymentId
// points at it. Both were wrong -- deploymentNodeSpec composes its id
// from its OWN args.deploymentId, and previousDeploymentId holds another
// deployment's row id -- so neither would be affected by dropping the
// mirror. Corrected in review rather than left to mislead the next
// person deciding whether the field can go.)
//
// Querying THROUGH the mirror is the defect; storing it is not.
// TestByIdQueriesFilterTheRowIdNotASelfMirror (dsl/row_id_mirror_test.go)
// enforces exactly that distinction, across the tree rather than here.
//
// Returns ONE row: the latest version. The comment here previously
// claimed this returned "EVERY appended row ... reconstructable asOf any
// point in time". That is not what the engine does -- executeFilterQuery
// runs loadLatestNodes (DISTINCT ON (id) ORDER BY id, "createdAt" DESC)
// plus a seen[id] skip, and nodesToMap / intersectNodeSets are keyed by
// id too, so every query result collapses to one row per id.
//
// That collapse is per-id AT THE ASOF INSTANT, though -- not unconditional.
// Given a timestamp the scan carries `createdAt <= T` and loadLatestNodes
// picks the newest version at-or-before T. So #1872's asOf-reconstructability
// criterion IS met: `asOf(deploymentById(deploymentId:"x"), "<rfc3339>")`
// returns the status current at T, and walking back via each returned row's
// own createdAt reconstructs the entire history using only the query surface.
// Proven against a live engine in
// component/memql/asof_reconstructability_1872_db_test.go (#2880).
//
// What is genuinely absent is the STRONGER capability of one query returning
// every appended version at once. #2880 read the collapse as defeating the
// criterion; it does not. If a timeline read is ever wanted, the prior art is
// a BUILTIN rather than a query directive -- see the harnessTrace rationale
// in dsl/harness/queries.memql.
//
// Do NOT add `asOf latest` here. It would change nothing about what this
// query returns, and would make the query unwrappable by a caller's asOf
// ("multiple asOf() directives are not supported"), silently removing
// point-in-time deployment reads altogether.
// TestDeploymentByIdRemainsWrappableInAsOf guards that.

/// The CURRENT state of one deployment, by deploymentId. Returns at most one row -- the latest
/// version. Status transitions append under the same id and a result collapses to one row per id,
/// so this is not a timeline read; for the state at an earlier instant, wrap it in
/// `asOf(deploymentById(...), "<rfc3339>")`. A one-query all-versions mode does not exist (#2880).
@unbounded("deployment by unique deploymentId -- bounded to a single row")
query deployment deploymentById {
  args {
    deploymentId  string!
  }
  filter  row.id == args.deploymentId
  shape   deploymentFull
}

// ---- supersededDeployments ----

// Deployment-aware reaper input (#1874). Returns ONE row per deployment
// (latest-per-deploymentId via `asOf latest`) whose CURRENT status is a
// terminal-not-active one -- superseded (a newer deploy promoted past it),
// failed, or rolled_back. These are exactly the deployments whose nodes are
// orphans: still-registered pods from a deploy that is no longer the active
// or in-flight one. The active-topology reconciler (TopologyReconciler,
// component/node/reconciler.go) reads this set every tick and immediately
// retires (health="stopped") any non-stopped v1:cluster:node whose
// deploymentId lands in it -- so a superseded deployment's nodes clear from
// topology within seconds of promotion completing, instead of waiting on the
// liveness lapse or the 30-min prune.
//
// Why status-driven (not "deploymentId != currentId"): keying off the
// deployment's recorded status avoids cutting an OLD deployment's pods while
// they are still in-flight during a rollover overlap -- a node is only an
// orphan once its deployment is explicitly marked superseded/failed/
// rolled_back by the deploy driver. A node whose deploymentId has NO
// deployment row (e.g. the dev "local" id) never matches, so local/dev
// clusters are untouched by the orphan path.
//
// Authz: filters on payload.status only (infrastructure topology, no
// user-scope field) -> conformance "other" bucket, same as
// nodesNotInDeployment / deploymentsForCluster.

/// Latest-per-deploymentId deployment rows whose status is terminal-not-active (superseded / failed
/// / rolled_back) -- the deployments whose still-registered nodes are orphans. Feeds the
/// active-topology reaper (#1874). asOf latest -> current status per deployment.
@unbounded("superseded/failed/rolled-back deployments GC sweep -- an internal sweep that must see all")
query deployment supersededDeployments {
  filter  status in ["superseded", "failed", "rolled_back"]
  shape   deploymentFull
  asOf    latest
}

// ---- nodeSpecsForDeployment ----

// A deployment's full per-node-type spec set (Epic 2 / #2094). Returns
// ONE row per node type -- the LATEST payload version per
// (deploymentId, nodeType) via `asOf latest`, so each entry reflects
// the node type's CURRENT spec (version / replicas / imageDigest). The
// deploymentNodeSpec store is append-only (one row appended per re-pin
// under the same composite concept id), so a plain scan would return
// every transition; asOf latest collapses to the current spec per node
// type. The caller passes the bare deploymentId (filter is on
// payload.deploymentId, NOT the canonical row id).
//
// Engine-as-spine resolution (empty version -> the deployment's engine
// version) is the CONSUMER's job: this query returns the raw per-node
// pins; the deploy pack resolves "" against the lockfile engineVersion.
//
// Authz: filters on payload.deploymentId only (infrastructure topology,
// no user-scope field) -> conformance "other" bucket, same as
// nodesForDeployment / deploymentsForCluster.

/// Latest-per-(deploymentId, nodeType) deploymentNodeSpec rows for one deploymentId -- the
/// deployment's current per-node-type spec set (version / replicas / imageDigest). asOf latest
/// collapses the append-only spec stream to current state per node type. Engine-as-spine resolution
/// of an empty version is the consumer's job. Epic 2 / #2094.
@unbounded("per-node specs for one deployment -- a bounded topology set consumed whole")
query deploymentNodeSpec nodeSpecsForDeployment {
  args {
    deploymentId  string!
  }
  filter  deploymentId == args.deploymentId
  shape   deploymentNodeSpecFull
  asOf    latest
}
